## GPU Sizing & Multi-Session Analysis (2026-08-13)

### Can we use T4 instead of L40S? — NO

| Spec | L40S | T4 | Ratio |
|------|------|-----|-------|
| FP16 TFLOPS | 362 | 65 | 5.5x |
| Memory bandwidth | 864 GB/s | 320 GB/s | 2.7x |
| VRAM | 48 GB | 16 GB | 3x |
| Cost (on-demand) | ~$2/hr | ~$0.60/hr | 3.3x |

**Why T4 fails:** On L40S at batch 1, per-frame inference is already 39.1ms — break-even
against the 40ms budget (25 FPS). The system only works because batch 4 gives 16.6ms/frame
(2.4x headroom to absorb CPU contention spikes).

T4 estimates (3-5.5x slower compute):
- Batch 1: ~100-120 ms/frame → **8-10 FPS** (unusable)
- Batch 4: ~45-55 ms/frame → **18-22 FPS** (below 25 FPS target, avatar freezes)

The dev's concern about memory bandwidth is valid — but even before bandwidth matters,
raw compute FLOPS rule out T4 for real-time 25 FPS lip-sync.

---

### Can we run multiple sessions on one L40S? — YES (max 2)

**VRAM is NOT the constraint:**

| Component | VRAM | Notes |
|-----------|------|-------|
| Shared models (UNet + VAE + Whisper + DWPose) | ~4.4 GB | Loaded once, shared across all sessions |
| Per-avatar latent tensor (1874 frames, 8ch, 32×32, FP16) | ~0.03 GB | Negligible per avatar |
| Working memory (inference activations, batch-4) | ~1.5 GB | Per concurrent inference |
| **Total for 1 session** | **~6 GB** | **42 GB free on 48 GB card** |

Even with 5 different avatars loaded, VRAM stays under 10 GB. Memory is not the problem.

**GPU compute IS the constraint:**

Each session at 25 FPS × 16.6 ms/frame (batch 4) = **415ms of GPU time per second**.

| Concurrent sessions | GPU time/sec | Utilization | Headroom | Verdict |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | 415ms | 42% | 58% (2.4x) | Comfortable |
| 2 | 830ms | 83% | 17% | **Viable — tight but works** |
| 2 (cross-session batching) | ~700ms | 70% | 30% | **Better — batching helps** |
| 3 | 1245ms | 125% | None | Frames drop, avatar freezes |

**With cross-session batching** (combining frames from 2 sessions into batch-8 instead
of two independent batch-4 runs), efficiency improves because larger batches amortize
kernel launch overhead. Batch 7-8 benchmarks at ~13.8 ms/frame vs 16.6 at batch 4.

**Cost implication:**
- Current: $2/hr per concurrent session (1 L40S per user)
- With 2 sessions/GPU: **$1/hr per session** (50% savings)
- At scale (e.g., 10 concurrent users): 5 GPUs instead of 10

**What's needed to enable multi-session:**

1. **Architecture refactor** — server currently assumes 1 avatar engine. Need to multiplex:
   - Shared model weights (already loaded once)
   - Per-session avatar state (latents, frames, blend params)
   - Per-session WebRTC tracks and audio pipelines

2. **Cross-session GPU scheduler** — batch frames from multiple sessions together:
   - Collect pending frames across sessions
   - Form larger batches (batch-8 from 2×batch-4)
   - Dispatch results back to correct session
   - Handle barge-in/session-end without stalling the other session

3. **Graceful degradation** — if both sessions talk simultaneously during a CPU spike:
   - Priority scheduling (first-connected gets priority?)
   - Dynamic batch size reduction
   - Frame skip with interpolation

**Risk:** Two sessions talking simultaneously is the worst case — both produce audio that
needs frame rendering at the same time. Idle sessions (listening) cost ~0 GPU. Real-world
utilization depends on talk/listen ratio (typically 30-40% talking).

**Realistic capacity with real usage patterns:**
- Users talk ~30-40% of session time
- Probability both talk at once: ~16% of time (0.4 × 0.4)
- Effective GPU load with 2 users: ~42% average, 83% peak
- Could potentially serve 3 users if willing to accept occasional frame drops during
  rare 3-way-simultaneous-talking moments

---

### Single container vs two containers? — Single container wins

| | Single container (multi-session) | Two containers (shared GPU) |
|---|---|---|
| Models in VRAM | 4.4 GB (loaded once) | **8.8 GB** (duplicated per process) |
| Cross-session batching | Yes — batch-8 = 13.8 ms/frame | No — each process batches alone at batch-4 |
| GPU utilization (2 users) | ~70% (with cross-batching) | 83%+ (no batching gain + context-switch tax) |
| CUDA contexts | 1 (efficient) | 2 (GPU time-slices, ~5-10% overhead) |
| Cold start cost | 1 × 27s | 2 × 27s |
| Code complexity | Higher (multiplex sessions in-process) | None (two identical containers) |
| Failure isolation | One crash kills both sessions | One crash only affects one user |
| Warm start on new session | Instant (models already loaded) | Full 27s (new container from scratch) |

**Why single container is the right architecture:**

1. **Cross-session batching is the key efficiency win.** Combining frames from 2 sessions
   into batch-8 drops per-frame cost from 16.6ms to 13.8ms — that's the difference between
   "tight at 83%" and "comfortable at 70%". Two separate containers can never achieve this
   because separate CUDA contexts cannot share a kernel launch.

2. **No duplicate model loading.** UNet + VAE + Whisper are loaded once and serve both
   sessions. Two containers load everything twice — wasting 4.4 GB VRAM and doubling the
   cold start time.

3. **Second session starts instantly.** With a single container already running, accepting
   a second user is just allocating per-session state (avatar latents, WebRTC track). No
   model loading, no warmup — sub-second. Two containers means a full 27s cold start for
   user #2.

4. **Simpler orchestration.** No need for NVIDIA MPS configuration, GPU sharing policies,
   or container-level resource limits. One container owns the GPU cleanly.

**When two containers makes sense:**
- During the transition period before multi-session code is built
- If failure isolation is critical (one buggy session must not crash the other)
- For A/B testing different server versions on the same GPU

**Recommendation:** Build toward single-container multi-session. Use two-container as a
temporary fallback only if multi-session code isn't ready but you need to serve 2 users
immediately (it'll work — just less efficiently).

